tomorrow marks the first night of  hanukkah the eight-day jewish festival  of lights a holiday that in part  celebrates the idea of hope of spiritual  resilience feelings which over the last  three years the jewish community in  squirrel hill and neighborhood of  pittsburgh have come to rely upon in  october of 2018 11 jews were murdered  while worshiping in a local synagogue  the horrific attack is the subject of a  new book squirrel hill the tree of life  synagogue and the soul of a neighborhood  we visited squirrel hill with author  mark oppenheimer to explore how the  entire community rallied illuminating  the idea of hope to overcome the  darkness of hate  this is ellesboro avenue this is where  my dad grew up mark oppenheimer can  trace his family history back three  generations here in squirrel hill  a pittsburgh neighborhood  that has held fast to its roots since  its founding around the time of world  war one i pretty quickly picked up on  the fact that there was something  special about this that it had had this  stable jewish minority for 100 years and  that it was it was unique in american  history while oppenheimer grew up  outside the area he was shaken when  squirrel hill became the site of the  deadliest anti-semitic attack in  american history  he's firing out of the front of the  building with an automatic weapon  on october 27 2018  robert bowers entered the tree of life  synagogue with three handguns and a  semi-automatic rifle  he's accused of fatally shooting 11  people and wounding six others  everyone killed was jewish  members of three separate congregations  using the same synagogue to worship and  i pretty quickly realized that  i had something of a calling to write  about this not just as a journalist  who's covered religion but also as  someone with a very personal stake in  this particular neighborhood oppenheimer  visited squirrel hill 32 times  interviewing more than 200 people for  what ultimately became his latest book  squirrel hill the tree of life synagogue  shooting and the soul of a neighborhood  one of the things that people pointed  out to me was that these ties both among  jews of different denominations but also  between jews and muslims jews christians  these weren't ties that they created on  october 27 2018 the morning or afternoon  of the killing that this was a  neighborhood of people who turned out  for each other not just online or not  just in phone calls but physically that  people bumped into each other at these  stores  at these little shops at these  family-owned businesses and hugged each  other and cooked for each other and  really did for each other not just in  digital space but in real life analog  space it's that neighborhood bond  oppenheimer details in his book like the  steps taken by strangers within the  jewish community to ensure religious  traditions were followed for the dead  despite the chaos that comes with a mass  shooting pittsburgh is very special in  that the squirrel hill jewish community  has a lot of cooperation between the  orthodox the conservative reform secular  jews all the different communities have  not just what you would call interfaith  cooperation but intra faith and jews  that's not always the case and that is  not always the case the book also  contains accounts from survivors like  rabbi jonathan perlman he escaped the  building but three members of his new  light congregation were killed it's been  over three years  but  for you personally does it feel like you  are that far removed from that day  no i i feel it's like it's uh  you know still with me  it seems like it happened yesterday you  know i look at the faces of  members of our school  and they  just don't know what to say but rabbi  perlman is consoled by memories of how  people of all backgrounds supported one  another in a time of need i'm glad the  testimony about  the shooting is out there for people to  read about  and the ways in which the entire region  can come together to say when you are  hateful towards one group within our  circle  we are going to repel you and we are  going to stand in solidarity with these  people make sure that they are protected  it's a message the neighborhood  continues to send with images created in  the wake of the shooting ever present  the non-jewish manager of the starbucks  got a non-jewish roman catholic friend  of hers who was an artist to come paint  jewish symbols in the windows this  stronger than hate symbol quickly became  synonymous with squirrel hill  oppenheimer dug into its origins and  found graphic designer tim hines you're  not jewish right i'm not jewish why did  you feel compelled to do something yeah  i wanted to show my support  that was something that was really  important to me was showing my support  and solidarity i started with the simple  image of  the steel logo  and uh you know swapped out a uh  uh one of those diamonds for for a star  of david and uh swapped out the steel  name and and added stronger than hate  heinz made the image available for free  it was downloaded more than twenty  thousand times  the only way that we're going to  rid ourselves of hate is to continually  spread acts of love and  whatever skill set someone has whether  it's graphic design or whether it's  the ability to clean up someone's yard  you know those acts of love go a long  way in battling hate the synagogue is  still not in use no one has prayed  inside it since that morning on the  fences still surrounding the tree of  life synagogue it is depictions of love  and unity that you find today  there's some people who want to go back  inside and use that same space as a  tribute to the victims there are other  people who feel that the best thing they  can do for the community is never use  that space again and reconfigure the  inside to do something different this  right now is so powerful obviously i  haven't been here before but seeing  the stars of david in the  in the building it's real it really is  impactful  a reminder of the hate that visited  squirrel hill but also of the hope that  followed what did you find in this  community is it stronger now than it  even was before  i don't know that you could measure any  community strength in such a way that  you could say it's stronger but it's  definitely as strong there are  communities in america that after a mass  killing have come apart at the seams  where people are at each other's throats  they're fighting over money they're  fighting over how to memorialize the  bodies they're fighting over what to do  with buildings there have been suicides  of survivors  and what you can say is that none of the  worst things that could have happened  have come to squirrel hill they managed  to dodge all the worst case scenarios  and i think that is a tribute to the  pre-existing strength of the  neighborhood ties  and just being in that community for the  day you really could feel this bond and  i know maybe that's not unique just to  squirrel hill that there are other  communities like that but it made such a  difference as the healing continues you  know rabbi perlman told me he hasn't  read this book he doesn't watch the  reports about it he is still in the  healing process as so many others are i  remember being there immediately after  and he is absolutely right what he  points out in that story is that people  came to support other people in person  right not just with phone calls or  online it was being there physically to  support them it's the fact that it  happened it's such a sacred place and  when that happens  you can't even touch the anger and also  the hope that we can get better in  making our world  more loving and that's what they want to  focus on is that hope that comes from  that moment of hate
 

